summary of the following resolution:

GENERAL ASSEMBLY, HUMAN RIGHTS COUNCIL (UNHRC) 
MAIN SUBMITTER: The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland
CO-SUBMITTER: The French Republic, The United States of America, The Kingdom of Belgium
SIGNATORIES: Czech Republic, The Republic of India, The Republic of South Africa, The Argentine Republic, The Republic of Finland, The Republic Of Chile, Republic of Costa Rica 

TOPIC:  Addressing Discrimination and Violence Based on Sexual Orientation and Gender Identity

The General Assembly,

Recalling past Human Rights Committee resolutions 32/2 of the 30th of June 2016 and 27/32 of 26 September 2014;

Bearing in mind its associated associated United Nations sustainable development goals; 

UN SDG 5: Gender Equality 
UN SDG 16: Peace, Justice, and Strong Institutions

Recalling the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and that it affirms all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights; 

Recalling also the purpose contained within the United Nations Charter which seeks to develop friendly relations among nations based on respect for the principle of equal rights and self-determination of peoples; 

Bearing in mind that the foremost motive of the United Nations, as stated in both Articles 1 and 55 of the UN Charter, is to promote universal equality and eliminate prejudice in all forms; 

Respecting all religions and cultures- understanding that progress in the promotion of elimination of discrimination against sexual orientation and gender may vary;

Emphasising that religion and cultural beliefs are not to be deployed as an excuse for violence and discrimination; 

Underlining that the current resolution should be adopted whilst ensuring the sovereignty rights of each country;

Acknowledging that non-governmental organisations play a crucial role in the promotion of addressing discrimination as the result of gender and sexual orientations;

Urging the emplacement of an education plan in regards to gender and sexual-orientation-based discrimination; 
The plan will primarily be targeted towards, but not limited to; 
Eastern Europe
Africa 
South Asia 
South America
The framework of materials being indoctrinated may include; 
The detrimental effects of hate crime against LGBTQ+ communities and genders; 
Current countries in contact with significant percentages of discrimination and hate crime; 
All qualitative data must be officially authorised and licensed by the proposed institutions called upon within operative clause 2;  
Advising all Primary and Secondary education systems to adopt the initiated plan into operation; 

Requesting funding from related NGOs in establishing surveilled research institutions; 
Predominantly calling upon the United Nations Entity for Gender Equality and the Empowerment of Women (UN Women) and the Human Rights Campaign (HRC); 
The aforementioned research institutions will have their principles set upon yet not limited to;
The accumulation of related data and statistics reported by member nations; 
The allocation of specialised agents for moderation of member nations in compliance with the policy put forth in operative clause 4; 
Dispensing finalised documents for the education systems within operative clause 1; 
Supply rational monetary funding towards areas in lack of finances; 



Encouraging the channelling of information in relation with sexual and gender-orientated discrimination through methods of advertisements; 
Soliciting that documents are to be transported towards nations in lack of knowledge or those in development as first priority; 
Calling upon member states to actively engage with the media to promote fair representations of the LGBTQ+ community and to detach stereotypes related to sexual orientation and gender identity; 
Structures of promotion may include; 
Social media; 
Television; 
Informative posters; 
News Sites;
Newspapers or Magazines; 


Advising all member states, to evaluate if their laws and the implementation of them are not discriminatory against people on account of their gender identity and sexual orientation;
Requesting moderation on countries in agreement with the policy to be made by authorised representatives from the initiated institutions; 
If laws are evaluated and reported as discriminatory, encourage the removal or replacement of such laws;
Accentuating all legislation in recognition as a form of hate-crime will be promptly abolished; 
Proffering the following to be implemented, however, is not obligated to; 
Nations to work in cooperation with others in order to foster the implementation of measures that contribute to the protection of all against violence and discrimination based on sexual orientation and gender identity;
Nations are to document yearly reports in relations with discrimination due to gender identity and sexual orientations for submissions to the institutions recognised in operative clause 2; 


Endorsing the establishment of social services in pursuit of enhancing gender and LGBTQ+ rights in countries; 
Social services will chiefly be devised within developing nations or those confronting high statistics of prejudice; 
Appealing to The UN Women and The Human Rights Campaign for financial fundings; 
Requesting all social service committees are to be led by official agents from the institutions called upon in operative clause 2; 
Objectives held by social services may include, however, are not limited to; 
Providing refuge to those facing discrimination; 
Supplying counselling to those who previously faced prejudice; 
Issuing job opportunities to those in lack of occupation due to discrimination; 

Strongly advising all member states to be cognizant of the discrimination occurring in the workplace; 
Countries are recommended to inaugurate a minimum wage in  which will apply to any and all genders and does not impede the basic human rights; 
Member nations are advised to invite authorised agents called upon within operative clause 2 to monitor official workplaces; 
Workplaces under surveillance may include; 
Factories
Company buildings
Primary and Secondary Education

Emphasising that all nations’ religions and traditions are to be recognized when discussing gender orientation; 
Encourage member countries to initiate interfaith dialogues with official representatives from religious groups, the UN Women and the Human Rights Campaign in prospect of bonding mutual respect; 
Understanding that religions are not to be utilised as a form to legalise hate-crime;

